        {
            "id": "32385293",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "17:12, 23 March 2024",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(9,416 bytes)",
            "change": 80,
            "summary": "(Added expert citations)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Grab a multimeter. A multimeter is a black box of electronic circuitry used to test electrical wiring or devices.<ref>[v162532_b01]. 14 March 2024.</ref> There is a large dial in the center of the multimeter where you can adjust the settings. The multimeter uses black (negative) and red (positive) leads to test electrical currency and resistance. These leads usually have metal clamps at the end of them. While the multimeter can seem daunting because of its many numbers and dials, you only need to use one setting for this test.<ref>http://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/how-to-use-a-multimeter/view-all</ref>[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find resistance value of your multimeter. Cross the two leads of the multimeter over each other and record the result. Make sure the metal clamps of the leads touch each other. If you are using a digital multimeter, then the reading will appear across the screen.<ref>[v162532_b01]. 14 March 2024.</ref>[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Grab a multimeter. A multimeter is a black box of electronic circuitry used to test electrical wiring or devices. There is a large dial in the center of the multimeter where you can adjust the settings. The multimeter uses black (negative) and red (positive) leads to test electrical currency and resistance. These leads usually have metal clamps at the end of them. While the multimeter can seem daunting because of its many numbers and dials, you only need to use one setting for this test.<ref>http://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/how-to-use-a-multimeter/view-all</ref>[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find resistance value of your multimeter. Cross the two leads of the multimeter over each other and record the result. Make sure the metal clamps of the leads touch each other. If you are using a digital multimeter, then the reading will appear across the screen.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
