        {
            "id": "18924068",
            "user": "Editrix",
            "timestamp": "14:37, 24 November 2015",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(8,718 bytes)",
            "change": 321,
            "summary": "(Removed an outdated method and made minor revisions based on expert review)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "reverted_by": "SarahB",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Glow plugs preheat the air in the combustion chamber in diesel engines so that they start faster when they are cold. If your engine is having trouble starting or you see smoke coming from your exhaust, then one or more of your glow plugs might be failing. Testing the glow plug yourself can help you avoid a trip to the mechanic.",
                    "#*Analog meters may apply higher voltage to the circuit than the digital meter, so use them with care.<ref>http://www.autoshop101.com/forms/h4.pdf</ref>",
                    "#*The first reading you get from a digital multimeter may not be accurate, since the current can fluctuate. Digital multimeters have a difficult time measuring current that is constantly changing.<ref>http://www.ebay.com/gds/Multimeters-Buying-Guide-/10000000177627218/g.html</ref> Because analog multimeters have a changing display, they can show you the current fluctuations. However, analog multimeters are less accurate overall.<ref>http://www.tester.co.uk/blog/electrical/digital-and-analogue-multimeters-which-is-better</ref>",
                    "#*It is best to use a digital multimeter for this test. A digital multimeter shows the exact number result of the test. An analog multimeter is harder to read because every possible result is listed at the top.<ref>http://www.ebay.com/gds/Multimeters-Buying-Guide-/10000000177627218/g.html</ref>",
                    "#*If you are using an analog multimeter, make sure to choose one with at least 20k ohm/V sensitivity.<ref>http://www.ebay.com/gds/Multimeters-Buying-Guide-/10000000177627218/g.html</ref>",
                    "#Test the voltage of the battery. Put your multimeter in DC Volt reading mode. Place the negative lead of your multimeter onto the negative terminal of the battery, and the positive lead onto the positive terminal. The reading should be close to 12.5 Volts when the car is off and close to 13 Volts when the car is running.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Locate the glow plugs. Consult the shop manual of your vehicle to find the location of the glow plugs in the engine. This will vary based on the make and model of your vehicle.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Look at the connector and pins for any signs of rust or corrosion and looseness. Take this opportunity to clean them.",
                    "#Evaluate the multimeter’s reading. Refer to your shop manual to determine the test specs for your vehicle.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 9.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Replace the glow plugs. If one or more of the glow plugs is faulty, replace all of them. Never replace a single plug.<ref>http://www.4crawler.com/Diesel/CheapTricks/GlowPlugs.shtml</ref> If the area around the glow plugs has dirt or buildup, clean it first.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 10.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Some manufacturers have special tools to clean out the bore where the glow plugs are inserted into the cylinder head. These tools clean out carbon buildup in the area inside the combustion chamber or precharge chamber. The tool also cleans the threads that the glow plug screws into. This tool is sometimes called a \"reamer.\"<ref>https://www.ngk.de/en/technology-in-detail/glow-plugs/installation-tips/removing-carbon-deposits-with-a-reamer/</ref>",
                    "#Set your multimeter to ohms. Use the lowest ohms setting. If the measurement of the glow plug is too high for the setting of the multimeter, then the plug is bad.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 12.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find the results on the multimeter. Check your shop manual for your vehicle's test specs.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 16.jpg|center]]",
                    "*Before you check the glow plugs, check the resistance from the negative battery terminal to the engine block. High resistance in the cable to the engine block will complicate your results."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "Glow plugs preheat diesel engines so that they start faster when they are cold. If your engine is having trouble starting or you see smoke coming from your exhaust, then one or more of your glow plugs might be failing. Testing the glow plug yourself can help you avoid a trip to the mechanic.",
                    "#*It is best to use a digital multimeter for this test. A digital multimeter shows the exact number result of the test. An analog multimeter is harder to read because every possible result is listed at the top.",
                    "#Test the voltage of the battery. Put your Multimeter in Volt reading mode. Place the negative lead of your multimeter onto the negative terminal of the battery, and the positive lead onto the positive terminal. The reading should be close to 12.5 Volts when the car is off and close to 13 Volts when the car is running.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Locate the glow plugs. Consult the owner’s manual of your car to find the location of the glow plugs in the engine. This will vary based on the make and model of your vehicle.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Look at the connector and pins for any signs of rust or corrosion. Take this opportunity to clean them.",
                    "#Evaluate the multimeter’s reading. The plug should read between .6 and 2 ohms of resistance.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 9.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Replace the glow plugs. If one or more of the glow plugs is faulty, replace all of them. Never replace a single plug.<ref>http://www.4crawler.com/Diesel/CheapTricks/GlowPlugs.shtml</ref>[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 10.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Set your multimeter to ohms. Set the range between 200-1000 ohms. If the measurement of the glow plug is too high for the setting of the multimeter, then the plug is bad.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 12.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find the results on the multimeter. The plug should read between .1 and 2 ohms of resistance.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 16.jpg|center]]",
                    "",
                    "===Testing with a Battery Charger===",
                    "#Remove the glow plugs from your engine. Check your owner’s manual to locate the glow plugs and to find the best way to remove them. This will vary based on the model and make of your vehicle.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 18.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Use a 10-12 amp battery charger. Using a charger within this range will provide enough current to heat the plug and avoid a false negative reading.<ref>http://www.4crawler.com/Diesel/CheapTricks/GlowPlugs.shtml</ref>[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 19.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Clamp the negative lead onto the plug’s body. Place the negative clamp from the charger onto the plug’s body.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 20.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Clamp the positive lead from the battery charger. Place the positive clamp from the charger onto the plug’s tip.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 21.jpg|center]]",
                    "#See if the plug glows. If it does not glow within seconds, then the plug is bad.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 22.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Do not leave the plug connected to the charger longer than necessary. A good plug can be damaged by doing this.",
                    "#*It’s possible for the plug to glow and still not heat your engine.",
                    "#Replace the glow plugs. If one or more of the glow plugs is faulty, replace all of them. Never replace a single plug.<ref>http://www.4crawler.com/Diesel/CheapTricks/GlowPlugs.shtml</ref>[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 23.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
