        {
            "id": "16268339",
            "user": "Wikivisual",
            "timestamp": "04:55, 1 January 2015",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(8,371 bytes)",
            "change": 1039,
            "summary": "(Saving new step-by-step photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Grab a multimeter. A multimeter is a black box of electronic circuitry used to test electrical wiring or devices. There is a large dial in the center of the multimeter where you can adjust the settings. The multimeter uses black (negative) and red (positive) leads to test electrical currency and resistance. These leads usually have metal clamps at the end of them. While the multimeter can seem daunting because of its many numbers and dials, you only need to use one setting for this test.<ref>http://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/how-to-use-a-multimeter/view-all</ref>[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Set your multimeter to ohms. The ohms setting will be denoted by a shape that resembles an upside down horseshoe.  There will be two long vertical lines containing the range of the ohms.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find resistance value of your multimeter. Cross the two leads of the multimeter over each other and record the result. Make sure the metal clamps of the leads touch each other. If you are using a digital multimeter, then the reading will appear across the screen.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Test the voltage of the battery. Put your Multimeter in Volt reading mode. Place the negative lead of your multimeter onto the negative terminal of the battery, and the positive lead onto the positive terminal. The reading should be close to 12.5 Volts when the car is off and close to 13 Volts when the car is running.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Locate the glow plugs. Consult the owner’s manual of your car to find the location of the glow plugs in the engine. This will vary based on the make and model of your vehicle.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Remove the caps or connectors to the glow plugs. There is usually a cover or cap over the glow plugs. Remove the cover or cap in order to apply the multimeter clamps to the glow plugs.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Clip the multimeter’s negative lead to a ground point in the engine. The main two ground points can be found by following the wire that leads from the negative battery terminal into the engine wall or following the wire that leads from the alternator into the engine wall.  These wires are attached to bolts. Attach the negative lead onto one of these bolts for grounding.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 7.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Connect the positive lead of your multimeter to the top of the glow plug.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Evaluate the multimeter’s reading. The plug should read between .6 and 2 ohms of resistance.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 9.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Replace the glow plugs. If one or more of the glow plugs is faulty, replace all of them. Never replace a single plug.<ref>http://www.4crawler.com/Diesel/CheapTricks/GlowPlugs.shtml</ref>[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 10.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Remove the glow plugs from your engine. Check your owner’s manual to locate the glow plugs and find the best way to remove them. This will vary based on the model and make of your vehicle.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 11.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Set your multimeter to ohms. Set the range between 200-1000 ohms. If the measurement of the glow plug is too high for the setting of the multimeter, then the plug is bad.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 12.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find resistance value of your multimeter. Cross the two leads of the multimeter over each other and record the result.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 13.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Place the negative lead of the multimeter onto the nut of the glow plug. Make sure you do no not place the negative lead higher than the nut.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 14.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Place the positive lead onto the tip of the glow plug. The tip is exposed when you pull the glow plug cap off.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 15.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find the results on the multimeter. The plug should read between .1 and 2 ohms of resistance.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 16.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Replace the glow plugs. If one or more of the glow plugs is faulty, replace all of them. Never replace a single plug.<ref>http://www.4crawler.com/Diesel/CheapTricks/GlowPlugs.shtml</ref>[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 17.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Remove the glow plugs from your engine. Check your owner’s manual to locate the glow plugs and to find the best way to remove them. This will vary based on the model and make of your vehicle.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 18.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Use a 10-12 amp battery charger. Using a charger within this range will provide enough current to heat the plug and avoid a false negative reading.<ref>http://www.4crawler.com/Diesel/CheapTricks/GlowPlugs.shtml</ref>[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 19.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Clamp the negative lead onto the plug’s body. Place the negative clamp from the charger onto the plug’s body.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 20.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Clamp the positive lead from the battery charger. Place the positive clamp from the charger onto the plug’s tip.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 21.jpg|center]]",
                    "#See if the plug glows. If it does not glow within seconds, then the plug is bad.[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 22.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Replace the glow plugs. If one or more of the glow plugs is faulty, replace all of them. Never replace a single plug.<ref>http://www.4crawler.com/Diesel/CheapTricks/GlowPlugs.shtml</ref>[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 23.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Grab a multimeter. A multimeter is a black box of electronic circuitry used to test electrical wiring or devices. There is a large dial in the center of the multimeter where you can adjust the settings. The multimeter uses black (negative) and red (positive) leads to test electrical currency and resistance. These leads usually have metal clamps at the end of them. While the multimeter can seem daunting because of its many numbers and dials, you only need to use one setting for this test.<ref>http://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/how-to-use-a-multimeter/view-all</ref>",
