        {
            "id": "16000794",
            "user": "Dr. Carrie",
            "timestamp": "22:10, 26 November 2014",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(7,289 bytes)",
            "change": 4473,
            "summary": "(significant expansion with multiple methods)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Glow plugs preheat diesel engines so that they start faster when they are cold. If your engine is having trouble starting or you see smoke coming from your exhaust, then one or more of your glow plugs might be failing. Testing the glow plug yourself can help you avoid a trip to the mechanic.",
                    "===Testing Glow Plugs in the Engine===",
                    "#Grab a multimeter. A multimeter is a black box of electronic circuitry used to test electrical wiring or devices. There is a large dial in the center of the multimeter where you can adjust the settings. The multimeter uses black (negative) and red (positive) leads to test electrical currency and resistance. These leads usually have metal clamps at the end of them. While the multimeter can seem daunting because of its many numbers and dials, you only need to use one setting for this test.<ref>http://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/how-to-use-a-multimeter/view-all</ref>",
                    "#*It is best to use a digital multimeter for this test. A digital multimeter shows the exact number result of the test. An analog multimeter is harder to read because every possible result is listed at the top.",
                    "#Set your multimeter to ohms. The ohms setting will be denoted by a shape that resembles an upside down horseshoe.  There will be two long vertical lines containing the range of the ohms.",
                    "#Find resistance value of your multimeter. Cross the two leads of the multimeter over each other and record the result. Make sure the metal clamps of the leads touch each other. If you are using a digital multimeter, then the reading will appear across the screen.",
                    "#*Subtract this amount from the glow plug reading.",
                    "#Test the voltage of the battery. Place the negative lead of your multimeter onto the negative terminal of the battery, and the positive lead onto the positive terminal. The reading should be close to 12.5 ohms when the car is off and close to 13 ohms when the car is running.",
                    "#*If this is not the case, check your battery or alternator before proceeding. Glow plugs will not work correctly if they do not have the correct voltage running into them.",
                    "#Locate the glow plugs. Consult the owner’s manual of your car to find the location of the glow plugs in the engine. This will vary based on the make and model of your vehicle.",
                    "#Remove the caps or connectors to the glow plugs. There is usually a cover or cap over the glow plugs. Remove the cover or cap in order to apply the multimeter clamps to the glow plugs.",
                    "#*Look at the connector and pins for any signs of rust or corrosion. Take this opportunity to clean them.",
                    "#Clip the multimeter’s negative lead to a ground point in the engine. The main two ground points can be found by following the wire that leads from the negative battery terminal into the engine wall or following the wire that leads from the alternator into the engine wall.  These wires are attached to bolts. Attach the negative lead onto one of these bolts for grounding.",
                    "#*Consult your owner’s manual to find the exact locations of the ground points.",
                    "#Connect the positive lead of your multimeter to the top of the glow plug.",
                    "#*If the multimeter's negative lead is still connected to the negative battery terminal, then you can leave it there.<ref>http://www.dieselgiant.com/glowplugrepair.htm</ref>",
                    "#Evaluate the multimeter’s reading. The plug should read between .6 and 2 ohms of resistance.",
                    "#*Subtract the resistance value of your multimeter from the resistance value of your glow plug. For example, if your plug's resistance measured .9 ohms and your meter's resistance measured  .2 ohms, the real reading for the plug is .7 ohms.<ref>http://www.dieselhub.com/maintenance/idi-glow-plug-troubleshooting.html</ref>",
                    "#*All the glow plugs in your engine should have similar readings. One glow plug with a higher resistance can affect your engine's performance--even if the plug is still good.<ref>http://www.dieselhub.com/maintenance/idi-glow-plug-troubleshooting.html</ref>",
                    "#Replace the glow plugs. If one or more of the glow plugs is faulty, replace all of them. Never replace a single plug.<ref>http://www.4crawler.com/Diesel/CheapTricks/GlowPlugs.shtml</ref>",
                    "",
                    "===Testing Removed Glow Plugs===",
                    "#Remove the glow plugs from your engine. Check your owner’s manual to locate the glow plugs and find the best way to remove them. This will vary based on the model and make of your vehicle.",
                    "#Set your multimeter to ohms. Set the range between 200-1000 ohms. If the measurement of the glow plug is too high for the setting of the multimeter, then the plug is bad.",
                    "#Find resistance value of your multimeter. Cross the two leads of the multimeter over each other and record the result.",
                    "#*Subtract this amount from the glow plug reading.",
                    "#Place the negative lead of the multimeter onto the nut of the glow plug. Make sure you do no not place the negative lead higher than the nut.",
                    "#Place the positive lead onto the tip of the glow plug. The tip is exposed when you pull the glow plug cap off.",
                    "#Find the results on the multimeter. The plug should read between .1 and 2 ohms of resistance.",
                    "#*Subtract the resistance value of your multimeter from the resistance value of your glow plug. For example, if your plug's resistance measured .9 ohms and your meter's resistance measured .2 ohms, the real reading for the plug is .7 ohms.",
                    "#*All the glow plugs from your engine should have similar readings. One glow plug with a higher resistance can affect your engine's performance--even if the plug is still good.<ref>http://www.dieselhub.com/maintenance/idi-glow-plug-troubleshooting.html</ref>",
                    "#Replace the glow plugs. If one or more of the glow plugs is faulty, replace all of them. Never replace a single plug.<ref>http://www.4crawler.com/Diesel/CheapTricks/GlowPlugs.shtml</ref>",
                    "",
                    "===Testing with a Battery Charger===",
                    "#Remove the glow plugs from your engine. Check your owner’s manual to locate the glow plugs and to find the best way to remove them. This will vary based on the model and make of your vehicle.",
                    "#Use a 10-12 amp battery charger. Using a charger within this range will provide enough current to heat the plug and avoid a false negative reading.<ref>http://www.4crawler.com/Diesel/CheapTricks/GlowPlugs.shtml</ref>",
                    "#Clamp the negative lead onto the plug’s body. Place the negative clamp from the charger onto the plug’s body.",
                    "#Clamp the positive lead from the battery charger. Place the positive clamp from the charger onto the plug’s tip.",
                    "#See if the plug glows. If it does not glow within seconds, then the plug is bad.",
                    "#*Do not leave the plug connected to the charger longer than necessary. A good plug can be damaged by doing this.",
                    "#*It’s possible for the plug to glow and still not heat your engine.",
                    "#Replace the glow plugs. If one or more of the glow plugs is faulty, replace all of them. Never replace a single plug.<ref>http://www.4crawler.com/Diesel/CheapTricks/GlowPlugs.shtml</ref>",
                    "*Remove glow plugs when the engine is hot. It's much harder to remove glow plugs when the engine is cold.",
                    "*Test any new plugs before putting them in your engine.",
                    "*Always wear safety glasses when working near your car.",
                    "What you need:",
                    "*Multimeter",
                    "*A 10-12 amp Battery Charger",
                    "*Safety glasses",
                    "{{reflist}}"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "Glow plugs are the heart of any diesel engine. Many diesel engines can start if one glow plug is bad. If two or more plugs are bad, your engine will not start. It is important to know if your glow plugs are in proper working condition. Continue at Step 1 to learn how to test glow plugs.",
                    "# Find the glow plugs on your engine.<br><br>[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#* Glow plugs resemble short metal pencils that have threaded ends. Usually there will be one glow plug for each cylinder in the engine and they will be on top of each cylinder.",
                    "# Remove all glow plugs for easier testing.<br><br>[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#* There is a way for testing glow plugs without having to remove them from the engine, however it is easier to remove them for testing.",
                    "#* Remove the electrical wires running to the top of the plugs. Do this by removing the small nut on top of each glow plug. Then, using a ratchet sized to fit your glow plugs, remove them from the glow plug holes.",
                    "# Use a battery charger to test the glow plugs.<br><br>[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#* Attach the negative clamp to the body of the glow plug.",
                    "#* Touch the positive clamp to the plug terminal.",
                    "#* Watch for the glow plug terminal to turn red from heat.",
                    "#* If the glow plug terminal does not heat up and glow red, then the plug is not working and should be discarded.",
                    "# Continue testing glow plugs in the same manner, discarding any plugs which do not heat up properly.<br><br>[[Image:Test Glow Plugs Step 4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "*Signs that your glow plugs might be bad are; difficult starting--especially on cold mornings--rough starting with visible smoke and not starting at all.",
                    "*Make sure, in addition to testing the old glow plugs, you test new ones as well.",
                    "*When deciding what battery charger to use in testing your glow plugs, look for one that ranges up to 10 amps. This should be sufficient amperage to heat up your glow plugs.",
                    "== Warnings ==",
                    "*Make sure you do not lose the small nuts. New nuts aren't included with new glow plugs.",
                    "*Make sure not to leave the glow plug attached to the battery charger any longer than is necessary to make sure it is in working condition. It may cause the plug to \"burn out\" and become useless.",
                    "*Ratchet set",
                    "*Small wrench",
                    "*Portable battery charger",
                    "*Glow plugs",
                    "*[http://www.4crawler.com/Diesel/CheapTricks/GlowPlugs.shtml http://www.4crawler.com/Diesel/CheapTricks/GlowPlugs.shtml]",
                    "*[http://www.askthemechanic.co.uk/electrical/testing-glow-plugs.html http://www.askthemechanic.co.uk/electrical/testing-glow-plugs.html]",
                    "[[es:probar las bujías de incandescencia de tu auto]]",
                    "[[it:Testare le Candelette]]",
                    "[[pt:Testar Velas]]"
                ]
            }
        },
